Mechanical Eyes and Machine Minds: From 19th-Century Photography to Generative AI




Part 1: Close Reading and Textual Analysis
The Facts of Photography as an Art Process



According to John Andrews, photography is fundamentally a mechanical process involving lenses, light, chemicals, plates, printing methods, and other technical procedures. 
He notes that photography can record extraordinary detail with a level of accuracy that no human hand can achieve. However, Andrews argues that this technical perfection can become a weakness because photography often captures too much detail and lacks the selective emphasis that artists use when composing a work. 
He explains that artistic success depends not only on machinery but also on composition, atmosphere, lighting, and thoughtful selection of subjects.
Ultimately, Andrews concludes that true art is not mere reproduction. Art transforms nature through human feeling, judgment, and imagination. 
Photography becomes artistic only when guided by an individual with artistic vision




The Character of the True Artist

Andrews distinguishes an artist from a machine operator through the possession of artistic feeling and educated judgment.
A routine operator simply records what is before the camera, while an artist studies nature carefully and seeks beauty, atmosphere, and expression. 
Andrews repeatedly emphasizes that photographers must learn to “watch and wait” for the proper light, weather, or moment. 
This practice reflects patience, observation, and intentionality rather than mechanical efficiency.
Artistic work emerges when the photographer actively chooses what is meaningful and beautiful instead of merely documenting reality.
For Andrews, the artist shapes the work through thoughtful selection, perseverance, and sensitivity to nature.

Photography in Service of the Arts

In Chapter 2, Andrews argues that photography should support, not replace, painting and other fine arts. He explains that photographs provide valuable records of details, landscapes, expressions, movement, architecture, and natural effects that artists can later study and incorporate into their work. Photography also helps reproduce paintings, sculptures, manuscripts, and other cultural objects for wider audiences. 
At the same time, Andrews insists that photography cannot replace the artist’s ability to idealize, select, and interpret nature. Instead, photography and painting are “sister arts” that strengthen one another.
Photography provides accurate records, while painting transforms those records into creative, imaginative artistic expression.
